Q1. What is Map in Java?
👉 Collection, Stores data as key-value pairs (unique keys).

Q2. Difference between HashMap and Hashtable?
HashMap → Not synchronized, allows 1 null key & multiple null values.
Hashtable → Synchronized, no null key or null value.

Q3. Difference between HashMap and LinkedHashMap?
HashMap → No order.
LinkedHashMap → Maintains insertion order.

Q4. Difference between HashMap and TreeMap?
HashMap → Unordered.
TreeMap → Sorted order (based on natural/comparator).

Q5. Can we use any object as a key in HashMap?
👉 Yes, but it must implement equals() and hashCode() properly.

Q6. How does HashMap work internally?
👉 Uses array of buckets + linked list/tree (since Java 8) → hashing + collision handling.

Q7. What is ConcurrentHashMap?
👉 Thread-safe version of HashMap, uses segment locking.

Q8. What is IdentityHashMap?
👉 Compares keys using == instead of .equals().

Q9. What is WeakHashMap?
👉 Stores keys as weak references → keys are GCed(Garbage Collected) when not referenced.

Q10. When to use HashMap vs TreeMap?
HashMap → Faster lookup, no order.
TreeMap → Sorted data, slower.

Q11. Why map is not a part of collection ?
Map is not part of the Collection interface because it stores key-value pairs, not individual elements. The Collection interface is designed for groups of single objects.



